+We propose a novel idea that makes the random term in the accumulator invariant throughout the loop. This prevents it from scaling, thus significantly simplifying the final correction step. Let bases $P_{1}, \ldots, P_{\ell} \in E\left(\mathbb{Z}_{q}\right)$ and scalars $s_{1}, \ldots, s_{\ell} \in \mathbb{Z}$. We will compute $\sum_{j} s_{j} P_{j}$ via a windowed double-and-add loop with w-bits windows. Let the prover select a random point $U$ and set the initial accumulator of the double-and-add loop to be acc $:=\ell U$. Also, define $V:=\left(2^{\mathrm{w}}-1\right) U \cdot{ }^{6}$ Process the window indices in descending order, $i=T,T-1,\ldots,1$. At window $i$, we double acc w times and then, for every $j \in[\ell]$, add $s_{j, i} P_{j}-V$, where $s_{j, i} \in\left[0,2^{\mathrm{w}}\right)$ is the $i$-th (little-endian) window of scalar $s_{j} .{ }^{7}$ After this, the random term in the accumulator becomes:
 
