 The full algorithm is described in Figure 4. The tables in Step 4 can be computed with incomplete addition by adding $P_{j}$ to the previous table element (the first being $-V$ ). It can be shown that all the preconditions of IncompleteAdd are satisfied across the table computation as long as $x_{P_{j}} \not \neq_{q} x_{V}$, which may be asserted explicitly, only once per base $P_{j}$. Similarly, multiplications by a small constant term like in Steps 3 and 5 can be mainly computed with incomplete addition. We denote by $-V$ the opposite of $V$, which can be obtained by negating the $y$ coordinate of $V$, i.e., as $\left(x_{V},-y_{V}, \beta_{V}\right)$, see Section 3.2 for details about foreign-field negation (over $\mathbb{Z}_{q}$ ).
 
